Systems that generate data sequences over time may undergo unforeseen alterations 
resulting in discernible changes in their output patterns, yet current methods 
fail to account for cases where these changes are only partially reflected in the 
data. To address this, we introduce a new framework that seeks to identify these 
transformations in real-time data streams, even when their impact is limited. Our 
approach not only detects changes but also isolates them, allowing the reconstruction 
of original data sequences from the modified ones. Furthermore, it provides an 
estimate of the alteration's occurrence time, even in partially observable cases. 
Through theoretical analysis and experiments involving synthetic and real data, we 
show that our method surpasses existing alternatives in accurately detecting 
partially-induced changes. Our work will be available via open-source on GitHub. 

